course: world
lesson: 53
title: Light, shadows and colours
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Light comes from sources such as the Sun, a lamp or a fire. The Moon is not a source: it only reflects sunlight.
explain: Light travels in straight lines. When something opaque blocks the light, a dark area with no light forms behind it — a shadow. Its shape matches the outline of the object.
explain: We see an object when light bounces off it and enters our eyes. A smooth shiny surface like a mirror reflects light neatly, so we see a clear picture in it.
explain: White sunlight is really a mix of all the colours of the rainbow. A red apple looks red because it reflects mostly red light into our eyes and soaks up the other colours.
explain: Materials that let all the light through, like clear glass, are transparent; ones that let some through, like frosted glass, are translucent; ones that let none through, like wood, are opaque.
fact: Light travels in straight lines.
fact: The Sun, a candle and a torch give off their own light, but the Moon only reflects sunlight.
fact: A shadow forms where an opaque object blocks the light.
fact: Moving an object closer to a lamp makes its shadow bigger.
fact: We see things when light from them enters our eyes.
fact: A mirror reflects light in a neat way, so it shows a clear image.
fact: White light is a mixture of all the colours of the rainbow.
fact: An object looks the colour of the light it reflects; it absorbs the other colours.
fact: A black object absorbs almost all light, and a white object reflects almost all of it.
fact: Light travels much faster than sound.
fact: Transparent materials let light through, translucent ones let some through, and opaque ones block it.
exercise: How does light travel? | In straight lines | In curves around objects | In circles | In zigzags like a snake
exercise: Why does an object make a shadow? | It blocks light, which cannot bend around it | It gives off dark rays | It pulls the light into itself like a magnet | The shadow is light reflected from it
exercise: Which of these is NOT a source of light? | The Moon | The Sun | A candle flame | A torch
exercise: How do we see a book on a table? | Light bounces off the book into our eyes | Our eyes send out rays that touch the book | The book gives off its own light | We see it only if it is hot
exercise: Why does a banana look yellow? | It reflects yellow light and absorbs the other colours | It absorbs only yellow light | It makes yellow light inside it | It changes white light into yellow paint
exercise: What is white sunlight made of? | A mixture of all the colours | Only yellow light | Only white light with no colours | Light and heat with no colour
exercise: Which material is transparent? | Clear window glass | Frosted bathroom glass | A wooden door | A cardboard box
exercise: What happens to a shadow on the wall when you move your hand closer to the lamp? | It gets bigger | It gets smaller | It disappears | It turns a different colour
exercise: Why do we see ourselves clearly in a mirror but not in a sheet of paper? | The mirror is smooth and reflects light neatly | Paper does not reflect any light at all | Mirrors make their own light | Paper absorbs all the colours
exercise: Why can't we see anything in a completely dark room? | No light reaches our eyes from the objects | Our eyes switch off in the dark | The objects disappear in the dark | Dark air is too thick to see through
exercise: Why does a black T-shirt get hotter in the sun than a white one? | Black absorbs most light, white reflects most of it | Black reflects more light than white | White cloth is always thicker | Black cloth makes its own heat
exercise: Which material is translucent? | Tracing paper | A brick | Clear water in a glass | A metal plate
exercise: During a storm, why do we see the lightning before we hear the thunder? | Light travels much faster than sound | The thunder happens much later | Sound travels faster than light | Our eyes are closer to the sky than our ears
test: A child stands in the sunshine. Where does the shadow fall? | On the side away from the Sun | Always towards the Sun | Directly on top of the child | Under the child, whatever the Sun does
test: Why can't you see round a corner without a mirror? | Light goes in straight lines and does not bend round it | The corner absorbs all the light | Your eyes only work straight ahead | Walls are made of dark material
test: A green leaf is lit by sunlight. Which is true? | It reflects mostly green light | It absorbs mostly green light | It gives off its own green light | It turns all light into green paint
test: Which object would make the darkest, sharpest shadow? | A thick wooden board | A sheet of clear glass | A piece of tissue paper | A plastic bottle of clear water
test: At night the Moon shines brightly. Where does its light come from? | Sunlight reflected off its surface | Fire burning on the Moon | Light from the Earth's cities | Glowing rocks deep inside it
test: Why does a cat need at least a little light to see in a dark room? | Seeing needs light entering the eyes | Cats see using heat only | Cats make light with their eyes | Darkness hides objects completely from all animals
test: A rainbow appears when sunlight shines through raindrops. What does it show? | White light contains all the colours | Raindrops are painted in colours | The Sun is many colours at different times | Clouds add colour to the light
test: You want to cast a bigger shadow puppet on the wall. What should you do? | Move the puppet nearer to the lamp | Move the puppet nearer to the wall | Use a dimmer lamp | Use a clear plastic puppet
test: why's my shadow huge when i'm right by the torch | Closer to the light, you block more of it | The torch makes you bigger | Shadows grow when it is cold | Light bends around you
test: why do black t-shirts get so hot in the sun | Black absorbs almost all the light | Black reflects almost all the light | Black makes its own heat | Black lets the light pass through
test: my brother says the Moon makes its own light. true? | No, the Moon only reflects light from the Sun | Yes, it glows like a lamp | Yes, but only when it is full | No, the Moon reflects light from the Earth's lamps
test: Sara reckons we see stuff cos light shoots out of our eyes. right? | No, we see when light from things enters our eyes | Yes, eyes send out beams | Yes, that is why it is dark at night | No, we see with sound bouncing back
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the illumination of the Moon? | The Moon reflects light received from the Sun | The Moon emits light generated within its own interior | The Moon reflects light received from the Earth's cities | The Moon emits light by reason of its volcanic activity
exam: It is given that an opaque object is displaced towards a lamp. Determine the consequent alteration in its shadow. | The shadow shall increase in size | The shadow shall diminish in size | The shadow shall remain of identical size | The shadow shall cease entirely to exist
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a given object is perceived as red when illuminated by white light. | It reflects red light and absorbs the remaining colours | It absorbs red light and reflects the remaining colours | It emits red light generated within itself | It transmits all colours save red through its body
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a material designated as translucent. | It permits the passage of a portion of the light incident upon it | It permits the passage of all light incident upon it | It prevents the passage of all light incident upon it | It emits a light of its own when illuminated
